---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { dots } from '../lib/patterns.js';
const stepsBg = dots();
const linesBg = dots();
import site from '../data/site.json';
const mailto = `mailto:${site.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Project inquiry')}`;
---
<Base title="AI systems for small offices" description={site.tagline}>
  <Hero variant="graph" tall eyebrow={`${site.legalName} · ${site.location}`} title="Stop being the filing system.">
    <p class="lede">A small office runs on one person's memory. Every email, text, and scan waits for that person to read it, file it, and write the reply. I build an AI system that does that work at your office and shows you, on your phone, the few things that need you.</p>
    <p>I'm JT Embry. Embry Development, LLC is my one-person studio in the Louisville area, backed by ten years of finance and healthcare engineering.</p>
    {site.booking && (
      <Fragment slot="actions">
        <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-book>Book a free consultation</button>
      </Fragment>
    )}
  </Hero>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Everything waits for you.</h2>
      <p>You are the one who knows where the quote is, what the client said, and what is due Friday. So you read every email, file every scan, and write every reply from memory. When you are on a job, in court, or on vacation, the office stops. A new hire has to learn the same pile from the start. ChatGPT on your phone does not help: it has never seen your files.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="field-agent">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Field Agent on your phone. Home base at your office.</h2>
      <p>Home base is a program on a computer you own. It reads what comes in, files it where it belongs, drafts the reply, and keeps every record indexed, so a question gets an answer with the source. Field Agent is the phone app that shows you what needs you today.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>From the truck, ask "What did we quote this customer?" and get the quote, with the document it came from.</li>
        <li>Ask for the follow-up email, and it is drafted from the thread.</li>
        <li>Open the app and see today: what to answer, what is due, what is waiting to be filed.</li>
        <li>Your records stay on your own computer, and nothing changes until you tap Allow.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>It works today. I run my own business on it. The system map shows every step, and a demo shows it best.</p>
      <p class="hero-actions">{site.booking && <button type="button" class="btn btn-primary" data-book>Book a demo</button>} <a href="/map">See the system map</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band band-parallax">
    <div class="band-bg" set:html={linesBg} aria-hidden="true" />
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What is inside</h2>
      <p>Four steps, the same ones my own office runs on. <a href="/map/journey">Follow one item through</a>.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Things come in.</strong> Email, texts, scans, voice notes, and the paperwork on the truck seat. Nothing is retyped. Paper and handwriting are read too, even <a href="/knowledge-assistants/transcription">old longhand</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>One in-tray.</strong> Every item lands in the same place on a computer you own, not in a vendor's cloud.</li>
        <li><strong>The system sorts it.</strong> It files each item, archives the noise, and drafts the reply. It follows written procedures, so it does the same thing every time.</li>
        <li><strong>The phone shows what needs you.</strong> One screen: what to answer, what is due, the next step on each job. Before anything is changed, the phone asks.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Two more pieces come with every install. The system <a href="/knowledge-assistants#rag">answers from your own files</a> and cites the page, so you can check it. Then <a href="/knowledge-assistants/ai-adoption">four weeks of training</a> beside your staff, so the office actually uses it.</p>
      <p><a href="/knowledge-assistants">What you get, in full</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Two side doors</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <p class="index">01</p>
          <h3>Custom software for any business</h3>
          <p class="problem">"We pay for five tools and still run the business on a spreadsheet."</p>
          <p>When your office needs a piece that does not exist, I build it around the way you work, with your leaders' input at every step. You own the code and the records. <a href="/software">How it works</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <p class="index">02</p>
          <h3>Rapid prototyping (CAD) and 3D printing</h3>
          <p class="problem">"We need a card, a tag, a part, or a short run that no catalog sells."</p>
          <p>Dog tags, business cards, cable clips, giveaways, and the piece that has to be this shape. I design from a sketch or measurements. You get the pieces and the files. <a href="/printing">How it works</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band-parallax">
    <div class="band-bg" set:html={stepsBg} aria-hidden="true" />
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How a job goes</h2>
      <p>Until recently, work like this took a team of developers. With AI tools, I do it alone, and fast.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Tell me the problem.</strong> One email is enough. I'll tell you whether I should build it.</li>
        <li><strong>Get a one-page scope.</strong> It says what you get, what it costs, and when, at a fixed price wherever I can scope the work.</li>
        <li><strong>See it every week.</strong> You see working software each week, not a reveal at the end.</li>
        <li><strong>Own it.</strong> You own the code, the files, and the parts, and you can take them anywhere.</li>
      </ol>
      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href={mailto}>Email me</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
